#### Problem 1
On Facebook, 20% of stories are "fake news". "Fake news" stories have a 40% probability of having at least 100 "likes". Real news stories have a 5% probability of having at least 100 "likes". If a story has at least 100 "likes", what is the probability that it is "fake news"? Please express your answer to the nearest whole percent (e.g. 65%).

_Answer_: 

$P(F) = 0.2$, $P(F') = 0.8$, $P(L|F) = 0.4$, $P(L|F') = 0.05$ 
$$P(F|L) = \frac{P(L|F)P(F)}{P(L|F)P(F)+P(L|F')P(F')} = \frac{0.4 \times 0.2}{0.4 \times 0.2 + 0.05 \times 0.8} = \frac{2}{3} = 0.67$$

